---
// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 fuddlesworth
// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
//
// /plasmazones/tiling/ — configuring autotile mode. The companion to
// /plasmazones/autotile/, which is the gallery of the algorithms
// themselves; this page is the behaviour around them.
//
// The support counts below are computed from the synced algorithm
// metadata rather than written down, so they cannot drift from the
// bundled set: only algorithms declaring supportsMasterCount /
// supportsSplitRatio actually honour those settings, which is why
// changing master count appears to do nothing on most of them.
//
// Also verified against source: the drag re-insert trigger shares the
// DragModifier vocabulary documented on /plasmazones/snapping/, and
// Tiling.Algorithm.PerAlgorithmSettings is written by the settings app
// rather than hand-edited.

import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHero from "../../components/PageHero.astro";
import Breadcrumb from "../../components/Breadcrumb.astro";
import Sidebar from "../../components/Sidebar.astro";
import GuideToc from "../../components/GuideToc.astro";
import PrevNext from "../../components/PrevNext.astro";
import { extractGuideHeadings } from "../../lib/toc";
import algorithmData from "../../data/plasmazones/algorithms.json";
import "../../styles/home.css";
import "../../styles/guide.css";

const toc = extractGuideHeadings(import.meta.url);

interface Algorithm {
    id: string;
    name: string;
    supportsMasterCount?: boolean;
    supportsSplitRatio?: boolean;
    defaultMaxWindows?: number;
}
const algorithms = algorithmData as Algorithm[];

const total = algorithms.length;
const withMaster = algorithms.filter(a => a.supportsMasterCount);
const withRatio = algorithms.filter(a => a.supportsSplitRatio);
---

<BaseLayout
    title="Configuring autotile — PlasmaZones"
    description="How autotile mode behaves: where new windows land, what happens past the window cap, dragging tiles, and which algorithms honour master count and split ratio."
>
    <main id="main" class="container guide guide-with-sidebar">

        <Sidebar section="plasmazones" />

        <article class="guide-content">

        <Breadcrumb />

        <PageHero
            title="Configuring autotile"
            tagline="Where windows land, what happens when there are too many, and which knobs your algorithm actually reads."
        >
            <p class="lede">
                Autotile places every window for you: open one and the
                layout redraws to fit it. The algorithm decides the shape,
                and the settings on this page decide the behaviour around
                it — insertion order, the window cap, what dragging does,
                and which windows are exempt.
            </p>
            <p class="muted">
                The {total} algorithms that ship in-box are in the
                <a href="/plasmazones/autotile/">algorithm gallery</a>,
                along with the Luau contract for writing your own. Exact
                defaults and ranges for every key named here are on the
                <a href="/plasmazones/settings/">settings reference</a>.
            </p>
        </PageHero>

        <h2 id="algorithm">Choosing an algorithm</h2>
        <p>
            <code>Tiling.Algorithm.Default</code> is the algorithm a screen
            uses until you assign it one of its own, and it ships as
            <code>bsp</code> — binary space partitioning, which splits the
            focused region in two for each new window. Per-screen
            assignment happens from the Monitors page, the same way
            <a href="/plasmazones/screens/">every other context assignment</a>
            does.
        </p>

        <h3 id="support">Not every algorithm reads every setting</h3>
        <p>
            This is the thing most worth knowing before you go changing
            numbers, because the failure mode is a setting that appears to
            do nothing.
        </p>
        <p>
            Each algorithm declares what it honours. Of the {total} bundled
            algorithms, <strong>{withRatio.length}</strong> honour
            <code>SplitRatio</code> and only
            <strong>{withMaster.length}</strong> honour
            <code>MasterCount</code>, because most of them have no master
            area for a count to mean anything about. A grid divides evenly;
            asking it for two master windows is not a question it can
            answer, so it ignores the setting rather than guessing.
        </p>
        <p>
            The {withMaster.length} that do take a master count:
        </p>
        <p class="chip-list">
            {withMaster.map(a => <code class="chip">{a.name}</code>)}
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
            <code>SplitRatioStep</code> is how far the increase and decrease
            shortcuts move the ratio per press, so it matters on the same
            {withRatio.length} algorithms and nowhere else.
        </p>

        <h3 id="params">Per-algorithm parameters</h3>
        <p>
            Some algorithms expose parameters of their own beyond the shared
            ones. Those values are stored per algorithm id under
            <code>Tiling.Algorithm.PerAlgorithmSettings</code>, so switching
            algorithms and switching back keeps whatever you set. It is
            written by the settings app; the shape is an implementation
            detail rather than something to hand-edit.
        </p>

        <h2 id="insertion">Where new windows go</h2>
        <p>
            <code>Tiling.Behavior.InsertPosition</code> takes three values,
            and it is the setting that most changes how the mode feels:
        </p>
        <ul class="links">
            <li>
                <strong><code>end</code></strong>
                <span class="muted">Append to the tiling order. The default, and the least disruptive — what you are looking at does not move.</span>
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong><code>afterFocused</code></strong>
                <span class="muted">Insert next to the window you are working in, so related windows stay adjacent.</span>
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong><code>asMaster</code></strong>
                <span class="muted">Every new window becomes the master. Familiar from dwm and its descendants.</span>
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            <code>FocusNewWindows</code> is on by default here, unlike in
            snapping mode, since a window that autotile placed for you is
            usually the one you just asked for.
        </p>

        <h2 id="overflow">The window cap</h2>
        <p>
            <code>Tiling.Algorithm.MaxWindows</code> caps how many windows
            the algorithm will tile, and
            <code>Tiling.Behavior.OverflowBehavior</code> decides what
            happens to the ones past it:
        </p>
        <ul class="links">
            <li>
                <strong><code>float</code></strong>
                <span class="muted">Windows beyond the cap are left floating. The default, and it keeps tiles from shrinking to slivers on a busy screen.</span>
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong><code>unlimited</code></strong>
                <span class="muted">Tile everything regardless of the cap.</span>
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="muted">
            Algorithms carry their own sensible cap as
            <code>defaultMaxWindows</code> — a grid that divides nine ways
            suggests nine — so the cap is not purely a matter of taste.
        </p>

        <h2 id="dragging">Dragging a tile</h2>
        <p>
            <code>Tiling.Behavior.DragBehavior</code> decides what happens
            when you pick a tiled window up.
            <code>float</code> pops it out of tiling into a free-floating
            window; <code>reorder</code> keeps it tiled and swaps it into
            whichever slot you drop it on.
        </p>
        <p>
            Separately from that, a trigger list re-inserts a dragged window
            into the stack at the cursor rather than at its slot.
            <code>Tiling.Behavior.Triggers</code> works exactly like the
            snapping activation trigger — the same
            <a href="/plasmazones/snapping/#activation">modifier vocabulary</a>,
            the same toggle mode, and the same release grace period for the
            case where a mouse-button trigger lifts a moment before the
            drop.
        </p>

        <h2 id="windows">Windows autotile leaves alone</h2>
        <p>
            A tiling mode that tiles everything is unusable, so several
            settings carve out exceptions.
        </p>
        <ul class="links">
            <li>
                <strong><code>RespectMinimumSize</code></strong>
                <span class="muted">Keep windows at or above the minimum size they ask for. On by default; it can leave visible gaps, which is the honest trade against squashing a window past usability.</span>
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong><code>StickyWindowHandling</code></strong>
                <span class="muted">What to do with windows shown on every desktop: treat them normally, only restore them, or ignore them entirely.</span>
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong><code>KeepFloatingAbove</code></strong>
                <span class="muted">Keep floated windows stacked over the tiled ones.</span>
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong><code>RestoreFloatedOnLogin</code></strong>
                <span class="muted">Put a floated window back where it was when it reopens after a logout.</span>
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong><code>LockedScreens</code></strong>
                <span class="muted">Screens whose tiling layout is frozen, as a comma-separated list of screen ids.</span>
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            For a specific application rather than a class of window, a
            <a href="/plasmazones/rules/">window rule</a> is the better
            tool: <code>excludePlacement</code> keeps one app out of tiling
            without touching the mode. Global size and transient-window
            thresholds live under <code>Exclusions</code> and apply to every
            mode at once.
        </p>

        <h2 id="gaps">Gaps</h2>
        <p>
            Gaps are global rather than per-mode — <code>Gaps.Inner</code>
            between windows, <code>Gaps.Outer</code> around the screen edge,
            with per-side outer values if you turn
            <code>Gaps.UsePerSide</code> on.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>Tiling.Gaps.SmartGaps</code> is the one tiling-specific
            piece: with a single window tiled, drop the gaps entirely so it
            sits flush to the screen. It is on by default.
        </p>

        <h2 id="next">Next</h2>
        <ul class="links">
            <li>
                <a href="/plasmazones/autotile/">Algorithm gallery →</a>
                <span class="muted">All {total} bundled algorithms with previews, plus the Luau contract for your own.</span>
            </li>
            <li>
                <a href="/plasmazones/settings/">Settings reference →</a>
                <span class="muted">Every key named here, with defaults and accepted values.</span>
            </li>
            <li>
                <a href="/plasmazones/shortcuts/">Keyboard shortcuts →</a>
                <span class="muted">Master ratio and count, retiling, and moving through the stack.</span>
            </li>
            <li>
                <a href="/plasmazones/scrolling/">Scrolling →</a>
                <span class="muted">The other automatic mode, if the window cap is the part you keep fighting.</span>
            </li>
        </ul>

            <PrevNext section="plasmazones" />
        </article>

        <GuideToc entries={toc} />

    </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
    .chip-list {
        display: flex;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
        gap: 4px;
        margin: var(--space-2) 0;
    }
    .chip {
        font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', ui-monospace, monospace;
        font-size: 11.5px;
        color: var(--link);
        background: var(--card-high);
        border: 1px solid var(--border);
        border-radius: var(--radius-xs);
        padding: 2px 6px;
    }
</style>
